faqs:
  - question: "Which providers does Appwrite Messaging support?"
    answer: "For email: Mailgun, Resend, SendGrid, Amazon SES and any SMTP server. For SMS: Twilio, MSG91, Telesign, Textmagic and Vonage. For push: Firebase Cloud Messaging (Android, iOS and web) and Apple Push Notification service (Apple devices). See the [providers docs](/docs/products/messaging/providers)."
  - question: "Do Appwrite users get an email target automatically?"
    answer: "Yes. When a user signs up with an email address, or you create a user with an email in the Console or the Users API, Appwrite creates an email target for them. Push targets are never automatic: your app must call `account.createPushTarget` after the user logs in."
  - question: "Why does my Appwrite message show failed when some users received it?"
    answer: "Appwrite marks a message `failed` if any recipient failed, even when the provider accepted the others. Read `deliveredTotal` and `deliveryErrors` with `messaging.getMessage` to see how many targets the provider accepted and why the rest failed. A failed message can't be sent again, so create a new one addressed to the recipients that failed."
  - question: "What does 'No valid recipients found' mean in Appwrite Messaging?"
    answer: "The message's users, targets and topics resolved to zero targets for that channel. The most common cause is a push notification sent to a user whose app never registered a device with `account.createPushTarget`."
  - question: "Can I send an Appwrite Messaging message from the client SDK?"
    answer: "No. `createEmail`, `createSms` and `createPush` need an API key with the `messages.write` scope, so call them from your backend or an [Appwrite Function](/docs/products/functions). Client SDKs can register push targets and subscribe a user's own targets to topics."
---

Appwrite Messaging sends email, SMS and push notifications through one API. A first send needs a **provider**, the service that delivers the message, such as Resend or Twilio, and a **target**, an address Appwrite can deliver to, for every person you message.

The API accepts a message before anything is delivered, so a broken setup looks fine until you check the message's status. Get one real message delivered from the Console first, then move it into code.

**Start where you are:**

- No project yet: create one in the [Appwrite Console](/sign-in), then [choose your first channel](#choose-channel).
- You know your channel and have provider credentials: go to [step 1, add a provider](#add-provider).
- You added a provider: go to [step 2, check your targets](#targets).
- Provider and targets are ready: go to [step 4, send a test from the Console](#send-console).
- The Console test worked: go to [step 5, send from a server SDK](#send-server).
- A message shows **Failed**: go to [step 7, read delivery status](#delivery-status).
- You've done all of this: go to [what to set up after messaging](#after-messaging).

# Choose your first channel {% #choose-channel %}

Start with email, even if push is what your app needs. Every user who signs up with an email address already has an address Appwrite can deliver to, so once a provider is connected you can message yourself from the Console without changing your app. Push needs a provider, client code that registers the device, and a signed-in user before the first notification arrives.

Get one channel working end to end, then add the others. Before the first send, each channel needs:

- **Email:** an account with Mailgun, Resend, SendGrid, Amazon SES or an SMTP server, and a sender address on a domain you verified with that provider. Do the domain verification first. It's the one part you can't finish inside Appwrite.
- **SMS:** an account with Twilio, MSG91, Telesign, Textmagic or Vonage, its API credentials, and a sender number or sender ID bought or registered with that provider.
- **Push:** Firebase Cloud Messaging (FCM) for Android, iOS and web, or Apple Push Notification service (APNs) for Apple devices only. FCM needs a service account JSON file. APNs needs a `.p8` auth key, its key ID, your team ID and your app's bundle ID.

If you ship on both Android and iOS, use FCM for both and upload your APNs key to Firebase. You then manage one push provider instead of two.

{% only_dark %}
![Create provider page listing the email, SMS and push services Appwrite Messaging supports](/images/blog/send-your-first-message-with-appwrite-messaging/dark/provider-picker.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![Create provider page listing the email, SMS and push services Appwrite Messaging supports](/images/blog/send-your-first-message-with-appwrite-messaging/provider-picker.avif)
