# Step 1: Add a provider {% #add-provider %}

1. In the Console, open **Messaging** > **Providers** and click **Create provider**.
2. Pick a service.
3. Fill in its credentials and the sender.
4. Click **Create provider**.

{% only_dark %}
![SMTP provider form filled in with host, credentials, sender name and sender email](/images/blog/send-your-first-message-with-appwrite-messaging/dark/provider-form.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![SMTP provider form filled in with host, credentials, sender name and sender email](/images/blog/send-your-first-message-with-appwrite-messaging/provider-form.avif)
{% /only_light %}

The [provider docs](/docs/products/messaging/providers) list each service's fields. The decision is which service to connect:

- If you already send email through a provider, connect that one. Your sending domain is already verified there.
- If you're on Appwrite Cloud and already have a domain verified in Resend, use Resend's **One-click setup**. Click **Connect with Resend**, pick the domain and click **Generate API key**. Appwrite creates a send-only key restricted to that domain and fills in the form.
- For SMS, compare coverage and pricing for the countries your users live in. You send through every SMS provider with the same `createSms` call.

Fill in **Sender email**, or **Sender phone** for SMS, before you save. If it's blank, Appwrite saves the provider as disabled, and every send fails with "No enabled provider found."

The sender must be on a domain or identity you verified with that provider. Two services also need the right region:

- **Mailgun:** the **EU region** switch must match where your account is hosted.
- **Amazon SES:** the **Region** must be where you verified your identity.

You'll know it worked when the provider shows **Enabled** in the **Providers** tab.

{% only_dark %}
![Providers tab with an SMTP email provider marked Enabled and a Twilio SMS provider saved without a sender marked Disabled](/images/blog/send-your-first-message-with-appwrite-messaging/dark/providers-list.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![Providers tab with an SMTP email provider marked Enabled and a Twilio SMS provider saved without a sender marked Disabled](/images/blog/send-your-first-message-with-appwrite-messaging/providers-list.avif)
{% /only_light %}

Enabled only means the required fields are filled in. Appwrite doesn't test the credentials when you save them, so the send in step 4 is the real check.

Keep one provider per channel to start. With more than one, targets that don't name a provider go through whichever one is enabled first, which makes failures harder to trace.

# Step 2: Check your targets {% #targets %}

A target is one email address, phone number or device push token that belongs to a user. When you address a message to a user, Appwrite sends it to every target that user has for that channel.

Whether a target already exists depends on the channel:

- **Email:** created automatically when a user signs up with an email address, and when you create a user with an email in the Console or with the Users API.
- **SMS:** created automatically when a user signs in with phone OTP, or when you create a user with a phone number. The number must start with `+` and have at most 15 digits, or Appwrite rejects it.
- **Push:** never created automatically. Your app has to register each device after the user logs in.

To test email right now:

1. Open **Auth** > **Users** > **Create user** and create a user for yourself with your own email address.
2. Open that user's **Targets** tab to see the email target and its ID.

{% only_dark %}
![A user's Targets tab listing a push, an SMS and an email target with their IDs](/images/blog/send-your-first-message-with-appwrite-messaging/dark/user-targets.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![A user's Targets tab listing a push, an SMS and an email target with their IDs](/images/blog/send-your-first-message-with-appwrite-messaging/user-targets.avif)
{% /only_light %}

For push, your app first gets the device's token. On the web, Firebase's `getToken()` returns it once the user allows notifications. On mobile, the FCM or APNs SDK gives it to you.

Then call `account.createPushTarget` while the user has a session, and save the returned target ID: tokens change, and you'll pass the new one to `account.updatePushTarget`.

{% tabs %}
{% tabsitem #push-target-code title="Code" %}

```js
import { Client, Account, ID } from 'appwrite';

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('<PROJECT_ID>');

const account = new Account(client);

// Run after every login. `token` is this device's FCM or APNs token.
const target = await account.createPushTarget({
    targetId: ID.unique(),
    identifier: token
});

// Keep target.$id to call account.updatePushTarget when the token changes.
```

{% /tabsitem %}
{% tabsitem #push-target-agent title="Ask your AI agent" %}

```text
Register this app's devices for Appwrite push notifications.
After every login, get the device's FCM registration token
(or APNs device token on Apple), then call
account.createPushTarget with ID.unique() as targetId and the
token as identifier. Store the returned target $id. When the
token refreshes, call account.updatePushTarget with that
target ID and the new token. Logging out deletes the target,
so register again on the next login. Follow
https://appwrite.io/docs/products/messaging/send-push-notifications
```

{% /tabsitem %}
{% /tabs %}

Two mistakes leave you without a push target:

- **Registering at app start, before login.** `createPushTarget` needs a session, so the call fails.
- **Registering once per install.** Logging out deletes the session's push targets, so register after every login.

You'll know it worked when the user's **Targets** tab lists a target with the provider type you're about to send on.

# Step 3: Choose topics or direct sends {% #topics-or-direct %}

Every message takes three recipient lists, `users`, `targets` and `topics`, and you can combine them. Pick by what the message is about:

- **One person**, such as a receipt or a security alert: send to `users`. A user's new devices and addresses are included without code changes.
- **One address or device**, such as confirming a newly added email: send to `targets`.
- **An opt-in group**, such as product updates or a weekly digest: send to a **topic**, a named list of subscribed targets you send to by ID.

{% only_dark %}
![Diagram: users own email, SMS and push targets, and a topic subscribes individual targets, so an email to a user and a push to a topic reach different targets](/images/blog/send-your-first-message-with-appwrite-messaging/dark/targets-diagram.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![Diagram: users own email, SMS and push targets, and a topic subscribes individual targets, so an email to a user and a push to a topic reach different targets](/images/blog/send-your-first-message-with-appwrite-messaging/targets-diagram.avif)
{% /only_light %}

To set up a topic in the Console:

1. Open **Messaging** > **Topics** > **Create topic** and give it a name.
2. On the topic's **Subscribers** tab, click **Add subscriber** and pick targets.
3. When you write a message, pick the topic with **Add** under **Topics**.

{% only_dark %}
![Subscribers tab of a Product updates topic listing four email targets and one push target](/images/blog/send-your-first-message-with-appwrite-messaging/dark/topic-subscribers.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![Subscribers tab of a Product updates topic listing four email targets and one push target](/images/blog/send-your-first-message-with-appwrite-messaging/topic-subscribers.avif)
{% /only_light %}

Topic subscribers are targets, not users. A user who subscribes their email target gets the topic's emails but not its push notifications, so subscribe each channel's target separately.

By default any signed-in user can subscribe their own targets with `messaging.createSubscriber` from the client SDK. To narrow that, set `subscribe` roles with `messaging.createTopic` or `updateTopic` from a server SDK, because the Console's topic form only asks for a name.

Keep private content out of topics, and send it to `users` or `targets` instead. For your first message, skip topics and send to yourself as a user.

# Step 4: Send a test from the Console {% #send-console %}

1. Open **Messaging** > **Messages** > **Create message** and pick the channel you connected. The Console creates a draft right away and opens it.
2. Fill in the subject and content.
3. Click **Add** under **Users** and pick yourself.
4. Click **Update draft**.
5. Click **Send message**, check the estimate, and click **Send**.

{% only_dark %}
![Email draft with one user added under Users, below the Schedule, Update draft and Send message buttons](/images/blog/send-your-first-message-with-appwrite-messaging/dark/message-draft.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![Email draft with one user added under Users, below the Schedule, Update draft and Send message buttons](/images/blog/send-your-first-message-with-appwrite-messaging/message-draft.avif)
{% /only_light %}

Save before you click **Send message**, because it sends the last saved version of the draft.

{% only_dark %}
![Confirm sending message dialog estimating delivery to 1 target](/images/blog/send-your-first-message-with-appwrite-messaging/dark/send-confirm.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![Confirm sending message dialog estimating delivery to 1 target](/images/blog/send-your-first-message-with-appwrite-messaging/send-confirm.avif)
{% /only_light %}

The confirmation dialog estimates how many targets the message will reach. An estimate of 0 means your recipients weren't saved. The estimate counts each user as one target without checking their targets, so for push, check the user's **Targets** tab first.

Drafts you never send stay in the **Messages** list until you delete them.

You'll know it worked when the message shows **Sent** in the **Messages** list and reaches you.

# Step 5: Send from a server SDK {% #send-server %}

Client SDKs can't create messages. Creating one needs an API key, so call Messaging from your backend or from an [Appwrite Function](/docs/products/functions), for example one that runs when an order is created.

Create a key in **API Keys** with the `messages.write` scope, and add `messages.read` for step 7. The [API keys docs](/docs/partners/project/api-keys#scopes) list every scope.

Install the SDK with `npm install node-appwrite` and keep the key in an environment variable, not in your code.

```js
import { Client, Messaging, ID } from 'node-appwrite';

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('<PROJECT_ID>')
    .setKey(process.env.APPWRITE_API_KEY);

const messaging = new Messaging(client);

const message = await messaging.createEmail({
    messageId: ID.unique(),
    subject: 'Your order has shipped',
    content: '<p>Your order is on its way.</p>',
    html: true,
    users: ['<USER_ID>']
});

console.log(message.status); // "processing"
```

The call returns as soon as Appwrite queues the message, with status `processing`. A successful response means the message was accepted, not delivered. Delivery happens in the background.

You'll know it worked when [`getMessage`](#delivery-status) returns status `sent` for that message ID.

Errors from this call are about the request itself:

- `message_missing_target`: you passed no users, targets or topics.
- `message_target_not_email` (or `_sms`, `_push`): an ID in `targets` doesn't exist or belongs to a different channel.
- `message_already_exists`: you reused a message ID. Generate one with `ID.unique()`.

# Step 6: Schedule a message {% #schedule %}

Add `scheduledAt` to any create call as an ISO 8601 date in the future. Appwrite stores the message as `scheduled` and moves it to `processing` at that time.

A date in the past is rejected, so build it from the current time. `toISOString()` returns UTC with a `Z` suffix, which keeps the send time unambiguous.

```js
await messaging.createEmail({
    messageId: ID.unique(),
    subject: 'Your weekly summary',
    content: 'Here is what changed this week.',
    users: ['<USER_ID>'],
    scheduledAt: new Date(Date.now() + 60 * 60 * 1000).toISOString() // in one hour
});
```

In the Console, save the draft with **Update draft**, click **Schedule** and pick a time under **Send at**. A scheduled message offers **Reschedule** and **Cancel scheduling**, which returns it to a draft.

You'll know it worked when the message shows **Scheduled** in the **Messages** list.

{% only_dark %}
![Messages list with a draft, a scheduled email, two sent emails and a failed SMS with a Details button](/images/blog/send-your-first-message-with-appwrite-messaging/dark/messages-list.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![Messages list with a draft, a scheduled email, two sent emails and a failed SMS with a Details button](/images/blog/send-your-first-message-with-appwrite-messaging/messages-list.avif)
{% /only_light %}

# Step 7: Read delivery status {% #delivery-status %}

A message moves through `draft`, `scheduled`, `processing`, and then `sent` or `failed`. The status that misleads people is `failed`: Appwrite marks a message `failed` if any recipient failed, even when the rest were delivered.

Read `deliveredTotal` to see how many targets the provider accepted, and `deliveryErrors` to see why the others failed. Accepted means the provider took the message, not that it reached an inbox, so check the provider's own logs for bounces and spam filtering.

In the Console, click **Details** next to the **Failed** badge to see the same errors.

{% only_dark %}
![Message error dialog for a failed SMS showing the delivery error No enabled provider found](/images/blog/send-your-first-message-with-appwrite-messaging/dark/failed-details.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![Message error dialog for a failed SMS showing the delivery error No enabled provider found](/images/blog/send-your-first-message-with-appwrite-messaging/failed-details.avif)
{% /only_light %}

```js
const message = await messaging.getMessage({ messageId: '<MESSAGE_ID>' });

console.log(message.status, message.deliveredTotal, message.deliveryErrors);
```

The delivery errors you're likely to see first:

- **No enabled provider found.** No provider for this channel is enabled. Add one, or enable it in the **Providers** tab.
- **No valid recipients found.** The recipients resolved to zero targets on this channel. Usually it's a push to a user whose app never called `createPushTarget`, or a topic with no subscribers on this channel.
- **Failed sending to target ... with error: ...** The provider rejected that one recipient. The text after `error:` comes from the provider, so look it up in the provider's docs.
- **Failed sending to targets with error: ...** The whole request to the provider failed, so nobody in that batch got the message. Check the provider's credentials and sender address.

A failed message can't be sent again. To retry, create a new message addressed only to the recipients that failed.

A push error ending in `Expired device token` means FCM or APNs no longer accepts that token. Appwrite marks the target expired and skips it on later sends. If a device stops receiving notifications, the app most likely got a new token and never called `updatePushTarget`.

[Messaging explained](/blog/post/messaging-explained) shows how the worker resolves topics and providers behind these statuses. Once push works, read [best practices for sending push notifications](/blog/post/push-notifications-best-practices).

# What to set up after messaging {% #after-messaging %}

Files you store in Storage can go out as email attachments, passed to `createEmail` as `<BUCKET_ID>:<FILE_ID>` with a key that also has the `files.read` scope. [Upload your first file to Appwrite Storage](/blog/post/upload-your-first-file-to-appwrite-storage) walks through the first bucket.

Once real users can trigger sends, for example through a public Function, protect that path. [Add your first Appwrite Firewall rules](/blog/post/add-your-first-appwrite-firewall-rules) shows how to rate limit it, which matters most for SMS, where scripted requests turn into a provider bill.

